#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133495 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ae774f-2496-56c0-a800-450bd8383092" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Inner Product of Two Complex Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "3263f466-0901-58a7-8cd9-ed62cd3036a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5545" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb C^3,$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}i \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}} \\\\  & =i\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-3})+(-2)\\cdot \\overset{―}{4+i}+0\\cdot \\overset{―}{5} \\\\  & =i\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot (4-i)+0 \\\\  & =-3i-8+2i \\\\  & =-8-i.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133496 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/id #uuid "b30bd921-7418-5a57-96eb-0c79dd5e4712" :question/math-academy-id "e-5547" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $\\langle \\,\\cdot\\,, \\,\\cdot\\, \\rangle$ over $\\mathbb{C}.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle\\in\\mathbb R$ for any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$\n\n- $\\langle \\mathrm{i}\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle =  \\langle \\mathbf{a},  -\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$ for any  $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$\n\n- $\\langle\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a},\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number for any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\alpha \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\alpha \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\alpha\\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\alpha} \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number, and $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0$ if and only if $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the linearity in the first argument  and the antilinearity in the second argument, we have\n\n$$\n\\langle  \\mathrm{i}\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle =  \\mathrm{i}\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle  = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\overline{\\, \\mathrm{i}\\, } \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a},- \\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{b} \\rangle\n$$\n\n \nfor any  $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, by the linearity in the first argument  and the antilinearity in the second argument, we have\n\n$$\n\\langle\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a},\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a} \\rangle =\\mathrm{i}\\cdot \\bar{ \\mathrm{i} }\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = -\\mathrm{i}^2\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle= \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle\n$$\n\n \nfor any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n.$ But by positive-definiteness, this is a nonnegative real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133604 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/id #uuid "8d5f83f0-dfb9-5437-a0e5-f33f68a59cec" :question/math-academy-id "e-8578" :question/problem "Given that $\\langle \\,\\cdot\\,, \\,\\cdot\\, \\rangle$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $\\mathbb{C},$ find $\\big\\langle -2\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}, \\: \\mathbf{c} \\big\\rangle$ if \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = 3+5\\text{i}, \\quad \\quad \\langle \\mathbf{c}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = 2-4\\text{i}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\alpha \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\alpha \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\alpha\\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\alpha} \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number, and $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0$ if and only if $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨-2a+3b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨-2a,c⟩+⟨3b,c⟩ \\\\  & =-2⟨a,c⟩+3⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2⟨a,c⟩+3⟨b,c⟩ & =-2⟨a,c⟩+3\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle$ and $\\langle \\mathbf{c}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2⟨a,c⟩+3\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩} & =-2(3+5\\text{i})+3\\overset{―}{(2-4\\text{i})} \\\\  & =-2(3+5\\text{i})+3(2+4\\text{i}) \\\\  & =-6-10\\text{i}+6+12\\text{i} \\\\  & =2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aab14461-fafd-5ce8-a827-b7fbcd8ba123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4+8\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333366} {:answer/id #uuid "632d4c5b-412e-5d54-8287-deafd0eb4f50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333367} {:answer/id #uuid "bbb1f13b-8ee6-5ffa-a10a-2c6c4e3eeb9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11+8\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333368} {:answer/id #uuid "5673cb0f-58ae-5230-9bed-341314357b1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4+11\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333369} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a10379-ceca-5e6e-8aa5-185c292e236c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7+6\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5673cb0f-58ae-5230-9bed-341314357b1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4+11\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333369} :answer-field/id #uuid "bccea3f0-485f-5dae-94e4-fcbdad249dfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0f409f0a-d1f3-5a6f-b4c4-c490f83517f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-141162" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨a,\\,2b+(3-2\\text{i})c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨b,a⟩=1-\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,c⟩=3\\text{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is antilinear in the second argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,\\,2b+(3-2\\text{i})c⟩ & =⟨a,2b⟩+⟨a,(3-2\\text{i})c⟩ \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{2}⟨a,b⟩+(\\overset{―}{3-2\\text{i}})⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨a,b⟩+(3+2\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2⟨a,b⟩+(3+2\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩ & =2\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+(3+2\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨b,a⟩$ and $⟨a,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+(3+2\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩ & =2\\cdot \\overset{―}{1-\\text{i}}+(3+2\\text{i})\\cdot (3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (1+\\text{i})-6+9\\text{i} \\\\  & =-4+11\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5a96f74-d810-5102-837c-d10677244aa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308186} {:answer/id #uuid "627e235f-736a-57a0-8c4c-b927d8a0a834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308187} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed76953-1190-5937-83a2-bdf61fdead78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308188} {:answer/id #uuid "e55e8215-30fc-5030-967d-854ae6eaffb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308189} {:answer/id #uuid "4eeaef49-611b-536d-b874-5f989b63e2b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ed76953-1190-5937-83a2-bdf61fdead78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308188} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c239fd8-ff0e-5201-985f-8ef8c181523a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d07c4830-0431-5d0d-83f4-02741718f7fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-97149" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,a⟩>0$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $a=0$ then $⟨a,a⟩=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the inner product in ${C}^{n}$ is linear in the first argument. So, a constant multiple from the first argument can be factored out.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "708e87bf-0009-52aa-a36d-60eb806f7f03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333373} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc6aff1-cee8-5a14-848d-6ce0a2bf9575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333374} {:answer/id #uuid "18a3c178-1ff7-5dbe-8f43-909f93b3cd04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333375} {:answer/id #uuid "05ac0162-68fd-579d-a436-b9959555b924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333376} {:answer/id #uuid "6b3075fc-47fc-505e-a92d-6e8cd526f02c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05ac0162-68fd-579d-a436-b9959555b924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333376} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0ec2b6a-0e60-5928-bcbc-9af9c861bb3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd73aa00-9e5a-5eba-8caf-b97d3032b2dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-97200" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨\\text{i}a,\\,b+c⟩$ if \n\n  $⟨a,b⟩=3-2\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨c,a⟩=2+\\text{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\text{i}a,\\,b+c⟩ & =⟨\\text{i}a,b⟩+⟨\\text{i}a,c⟩ \\\\  & =\\text{i}⟨a,b⟩+\\text{i}⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}⟨a,b⟩+\\text{i}⟨a,c⟩ & =\\text{i}⟨a,b⟩+\\text{i}\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨a,b⟩$ and $⟨c,a⟩:$    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}⟨a,b⟩+\\text{i}\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩} & =\\text{i}(3-2\\text{i})+\\text{i}\\overset{―}{(2+\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\text{i}(3-2\\text{i})+\\text{i}(2-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =2+3\\text{i}+1+2\\text{i} \\\\  & =3+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf518d7a-01f5-50d2-b94f-65e8a488c869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308214} {:answer/id #uuid "49b4ef0e-f1e5-54ed-ba18-bbe54250378c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308215} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f74b48-910b-58f3-8e92-bba56a87d7ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308216} {:answer/id #uuid "7719dd6e-1c7d-57be-820b-737fe5ab28e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308217} {:answer/id #uuid "9344d692-5295-53a1-bdc1-184b52e08273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9344d692-5295-53a1-bdc1-184b52e08273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308218} :answer-field/id #uuid "e34b21cc-e704-5a61-9eb7-a3fda73d7473" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa324a2f-2ed0-54ed-bd7c-536b84e60017" :question/math-academy-id "q-97209" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨4a-b,\\,c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨c,a⟩=1-2\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨b,c⟩=2+3\\text{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨4a-b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨4a,c⟩-⟨b,c⟩ \\\\  & =4⟨a,c⟩-⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4⟨a,c⟩-⟨b,c⟩ & =4\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}-⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨c,a⟩$ and $⟨b,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}-⟨b,c⟩ & =4\\overset{―}{(1-2\\text{i})}-(2+3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =4(1+2\\text{i})-(2+3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =4+8\\text{i}-2-3\\text{i} \\\\  & =2+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78ae8a6e-64c5-5c6d-9246-4ac0eb3d4eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308228} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f96905-f6cd-53ad-88cd-7c8551c2948d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308229} {:answer/id #uuid "f0551a2f-4a60-58cb-a598-26b021d36a7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308230} {:answer/id #uuid "dafb893a-2fed-57f4-b32e-01a16b0f6572" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308231} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb83b1e-f1a8-563d-b597-1ea16704e11d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0551a2f-4a60-58cb-a598-26b021d36a7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308230} :answer-field/id #uuid "84213374-f1b5-54a9-ba3b-3c509c124fda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5739baad-8a41-53ea-98cf-d058e0e17b20" :question/math-academy-id "q-97248" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,-ia⟩=-i⟨a,a⟩$ for any nonzero vector  $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, by the antilinearity in the second argument we have\n\n$$\n⟨a,-ia⟩=\\overset{―}{(-i)}⟨a,a⟩=i⟨a,a⟩\\ne -i⟨a,a⟩\n$$\n\n \nfor any nonzero vector  $a\\in {C}^{n}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4afe5cc5-6a3f-5f9c-a3fb-fa76e06d93ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308242} {:answer/id #uuid "74419b3d-b13f-57c7-9e9f-07c716d35a99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308243} {:answer/id #uuid "b3460bc8-25ee-503c-8066-b2decb3a421a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308244} {:answer/id #uuid "2dc0aed1-8969-50df-80c0-ac150ad1f5db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308245} {:answer/id #uuid "da903bef-5d47-524e-b08e-ec3f5446ac3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da903bef-5d47-524e-b08e-ec3f5446ac3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308246} :answer-field/id #uuid "49e42b05-211a-582f-9fa8-59379f469326" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6174044c-c49a-50d3-8871-9332394846b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-97296" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨a,b⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $i⟨a,b⟩=⟨a,-ib⟩$ for any  $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- There is vector $a\\in {C}^{n}$ such that  $⟨a,a⟩<0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. A complex number is equal to its conjugate if and only if it is a real number. The inner product over $C$  is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,-ib⟩=\\overset{―}{(-i)}⟨a,b⟩=i⟨a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false.  The inner product over $C$ is positive-definite. So, for any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
